WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.464
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.229
JONATHAN SHRIVER-BLAKE: Hello.

00:00:09.229 --> 00:00:10.020
Welcome, everybody.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.400
Thanks for coming out.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.600
My name is John
Shriver-Blake, and I'm

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.170
a product manager for Firebase.

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.020
And I'll be joined later today
by Mike McDonald, who's also

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.090
a Firebase product manager.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.550
And we're here today
to talk about building

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.670
enterprise-grade mobile
apps with Firebase.

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.970
You see, lots of people know
that Firebase is a great way

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.480
to build great apps.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.990
And that helps you throughout
the lifecycle of your app

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:34.339
development process.

00:00:34.339 --> 00:00:35.630
It can help you build your app.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.280
With features like
Firestore, Cloud Storage,

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.740
and Authentication,
you can store and sync

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.020
data in the cloud
for your mobile app

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.240
without running your
own backend services.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.790
You can then build on top
of that functionality using

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:53.420
Cloud Functions to run
backend code, once again,

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.190
without your own servers.

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.900
Finally, you can use Firebase
to help you take advantage of ML

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.320
in your app, using ML
Kit and both client

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.180
and cloud-side APIs.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.790
Beyond building
your app, Firebase

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.590
can help you with
your app quality.

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.830
You can use Crashlytics to get
a real-time feed of your crashes

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:15.350
as they happen in the wild.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.480
You can then use
Crashlytics to prioritize

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.740
and help fix those issues.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.730
You can also get a clear idea
of your app's performance

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.010
and how it's being
perceived by your users,

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.050
and improve upon
your performance

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:29.180
with Firebase
Performance Monitoring.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.360
Finally, you can run
tests for your app

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:37.100
on devices managed by Google
with Firebase Test Lab.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.600
Beyond quality, we can
help you grow your app.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.960
This starts with Google
Analytics for Firebase,

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.370
which gives you free
unlimited app analytics

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.640
so you can have a clear
understanding of what

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.250
your users are
doing in your app.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:50.930
Once you have that
understanding,

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.700
you can then optimize
that experience

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.420
using A/B testing, Firebase
Cloud Messaging, and Remote

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.870
Config to really get
your users more engaged

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:01.730
and using more of your app.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.830
Finally, we have
predictions, which

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:07.460
uses ML to analyze
your user's behavior

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:11.130
and creates custom groups so
you can then target those users.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.390
For instance, there is a
custom group for retention

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.330
that will give you an idea of
users who are likely to churn.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.000
You can then use notifications
or AB testing or Remote

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.920
Config to create specific
experiences for those users

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.560
to keep them from
churning out of your app.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.980
There really is a lot that
Firebase has to offer.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.190
The problem is many people
think that Firebase can't be

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.260
used in an enterprise setting.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.570
And so I get
questions all the time

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:42.260
about security, compliance,
privacy, and production

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.850
readiness for Firebase.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:47.240
And so today we're going to
answer some of those questions

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.270
and give you the information
that you need to get Firebase

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.390
deployed in your organization.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:56.120
Specifically, we're going
to cover these three areas.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.610
Is Firebase ready for
your organization?

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.490
Can you manage your
resources in Firebase

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:04.460
and control access in
the way that you need to?

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.620
Is Firebase ready
for your lawyers?

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.830
Will it meet your regulatory
and compliance requirements?

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:12.650
And finally, is Firebase
ready for production?

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.010
Will it scale?

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:17.700
Can you monitor it in
the way that you expect?

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.150
So first let's talk
about your organization.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.220
Can you manage
and control access

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.730
to Firebase projects in
a way that you need to?

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:27.440
The answer is yes.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.040
Firebase users can use Google
Cloud's enterprise-grade tools

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.210
to manage and control access
to Firebase resources, which

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.800
is great, right?

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.950
And this is possible because,
when it comes right down to it,

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:42.470
Firebase projects
are Cloud projects.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.450
Now we in the Firebase
team have often

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.960
built tools to help users get
onto the Firebase platform

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.820
in an easy and simple way.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.580
But underlying all of it
is Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.770
And so users who
want to can take

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:57.890
advantage of the more powerful
Google Cloud Platform tools

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.620
to perform more advanced
administrative scenarios.

00:04:02.917 --> 00:04:04.750
Let's talk about some
of the tools the cloud

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.330
makes available for
this type of management.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:10.660
The first is Google
Cloud Resource Manager.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:14.320
This is your central way to
manage cloud and Firebase

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.852
resources within
your organization.

00:04:16.852 --> 00:04:18.310
It gives administrators
the ability

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.980
to view all of the projects
and resources deployed

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:23.500
in their org, to
organize those projects

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.480
in a hierarchy of
folders, and then

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:29.590
assign access at the org,
folder, project, and resource

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.160
level.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.500
Second is cloud identity.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:35.870
This is your centralized
identity management solution

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:36.860
for cloud.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.440
It allows you to manage
your users and groups,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.260
and supports products like
single sign-on and two-factor

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.390
authentication so
you can be sure

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:47.360
that your users are having
both a safe and secure user

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.190
experience.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:54.720
Finally is Cloud IAM, which is
Identity and Access Management.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.270
This gives you
fine-grained access control

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.420
of who has access to what
in your organization.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.090
You do this by assigning
roles to specific users

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:07.020
and groups that give them
limited access within Cloud.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.570
For instance, there's a role
called App Engine Viewer

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:12.905
which gives users the ability to
view App Engine configuration.

00:05:12.905 --> 00:05:15.810
Now this is a fairly broad role
so you might assign it fairly

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.970
broadly, let's say, to a group.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:20.400
On the other hand, you might
reserve more of an edit role

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.800
to a smaller set of people.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.710
Cloud IAM puts you in
that level of control.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.120
Now those are predefined roles.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.190
Something that Cloud IAM also
supports are custom roles.

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:34.260
And so if you find that the
predefined roles that we've

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.140
defined are not
sufficient for your needs,

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.319
you can get really
granular and create roles

00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:40.860
with exactly the
permissions that you

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.277
need for your organization.

00:05:43.277 --> 00:05:45.360
Finally, I want to mention
that all changes to IAM

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.470
are audited and logged so
that you can go back and get

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:53.150
a clear view of what changes
were made, when, and by whom

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:55.320
in your project.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.620
Now those of you who are
familiar Firebase probably

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:00.380
know that today we don't
offer a lot of options

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:03.050
for fine-grained access
control with Firebase.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.630
Well, good news--
rolling out now

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:07.912
is fine-grained access
control for Firebase.

00:06:07.912 --> 00:06:09.620
This is actually
available to some of you

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:11.540
today, and will be
broadly available

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.490
by the end of the summer.

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.560
We've defined a set
of predefined roles

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:19.730
for Firebase users, like quality
admin or developer viewer, that

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:23.180
allow you to very easily give
people in Firebase access

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:26.760
to a limited subset
of the functionality.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.450
In addition, we'll be
supporting custom roles.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:32.450
So you can create your own roles
with exactly the permissions

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:35.100
that make the most sense
for your organization.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.554
Now this is all rolling
out now, but I'm

00:06:37.554 --> 00:06:39.470
going to give you a
preview of what it'll look

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:43.350
like so you've got a good idea.

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.910
OK, so here I am in
the Firebase console.

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:48.680
You can see I'm logged in as me.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.970
And I want to go ahead and
give Mike access to my project

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:54.370
so that he can
send notifications.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.470
So I'm going to
go into Settings,

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:58.540
and this brings up a
Firebase-specific user

00:06:58.540 --> 00:06:59.760
management UI.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.005
I'm going to go
ahead and add Mike.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:06.700
There we go.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.500
And I'm going to select a role.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:10.210
And you can see in
the Role dropdown

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:12.610
there's the project level
roles that Firebase has always

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:13.640
supported.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.420
But I can also give Mike
custom access to Firebase.

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:19.660
And somebody who's going to
wear shorts to a presentation,

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:21.860
I'm not so sure I trust
their professionalism.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.640
So I'm going to lock down Mike
as much as possible by giving

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:27.400
him only grow admin access,
which means he can access

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.100
notifications but can't
access for instance

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:33.360
the contents of my database.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.425
So there we go.

00:07:34.425 --> 00:07:35.630
I'm going to add Mike.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:38.960
Cool.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:40.820
So let me switch over to Mike.

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.700
You can see here I am
logged in as Firebase Mike.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:47.504
And because Mike has
grow admin access--

00:07:47.504 --> 00:07:48.920
so if I go to Cloud
Messaging, you

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:51.101
can see he has full
read-write access.

00:07:51.101 --> 00:07:57.330
But if I go to
Database, access denied.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:00.115
He needs to ask a project owner
for the necessary permissions.

00:08:00.115 --> 00:08:01.490
So I've been able
to grant access

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.734
Mike to only the things
that he needs, and I don't

00:08:03.734 --> 00:08:05.400
have to worry about
him accessing things

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.410
that he shouldn't be.

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.290
Now some of you may be thinking
that these are a little too

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:11.760
coarse-grain for you.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:15.020
And for those folks, you
can use custom roles.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.270
So switching back to me,
once again logged in as me,

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.990
I'm going to go ahead and show
you how you do a custom role.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:23.210
Now this is not something
that we support in our UI

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:25.190
because it's more of an
advanced user scenario.

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:27.810
So I'm going to go ahead and
click Advanced Permissions.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:31.130
What this does is open up the
full Google Cloud Platform

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:34.409
console and the full
IAM experience there.

00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:35.888
And if I go into
the flyout here,

00:08:35.888 --> 00:08:37.429
you can see that
there's really a lot

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:39.650
of additional functionality
that's available here

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:42.049
for folks who want to
take advantage of it.

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:44.704
One of those things are roles.

00:08:44.704 --> 00:08:46.370
So as I load this up,
you'll see there's

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.470
a long list of the
predefined roles.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:49.970
But in addition to
those roles, I've

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:52.406
created a special
role just for Mike

00:08:52.406 --> 00:08:53.780
that gives him
exactly the access

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:56.097
that he needs and nothing else.

00:08:56.097 --> 00:08:57.680
So there's really a
lot of flexibility

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.530
here for those who need it.

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:02.742
Finally, I mentioned
audit login.

00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:05.990
So I'm quickly move over to the
Google Cloud Platform activity

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:08.307
stream, which is a
central place to see

00:09:08.307 --> 00:09:09.890
all of the administrative
actions that

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:16.030
have happened in your
organization, including IAM.

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:17.080
And it's a little slow.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:20.560
It's the Wi-Fi.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:21.420
I'm going to blame the Wi-Fi.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:21.919
Here we go.

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:25.210
And there it is.

00:09:25.210 --> 00:09:27.386
So not only do I
have a full activity

00:09:27.386 --> 00:09:29.260
view of all of the
changes that have happened

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.660
in my organization, it
also reflects the change

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:32.800
that I just made.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:37.370
Firebase John assigned Firebase
growth admin to Firebase Mike.

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:40.060
So I can get that full view
of all the changes happening

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.490
in my org, which is
super important to me

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:45.760
as a project administrator.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:50.350
So going back to the deck,
the key takeaway for you

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:54.400
for this section is that you
can use the same powerful tools

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:56.410
available in Google
Cloud Platform

00:09:56.410 --> 00:10:01.500
to manage and control access
to your Firebase resources.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:04.220
So let's talk about our lawyers.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:06.210
Anybody who's ever
deployed a new service

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.670
in a large organization
knows that it

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.400
can be a real challenge,
particularly because

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:12.570
of the lawyers.

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.430
If a service doesn't
meet your compliance

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:18.150
and regulatory needs, it can
be a complete nonstarter.

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.930
In addition, GDPR and other
emergent privacy regulations

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.060
have made it this
much more complicated.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:27.510
We in Firebase want to
make this easier for you,

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.330
and we've been doing a
lot of work to that end.

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:32.310
And we're happy to say
that today Firebase

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:34.470
is ready to meet most
organization's compliance

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:39.760
needs, including
helping you in GDPR.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.180
Now before I get
too much further,

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:42.596
I want to make
this really clear--

00:10:42.596 --> 00:10:45.260
I am not a lawyer and
this is not legal advice.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.950
If you haven't discussed some of
these topics with a lawyer yet,

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:49.300
I strongly suggest that you do.

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:55.990
GDPR.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:58.510
So I had a interesting
experience this past winter.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:01.500
I was at a water
park with my son,

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:03.250
and we were waiting
in this very long line

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:04.720
to get in the water park.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:07.030
And all of a sudden,
a couple behind me

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:08.770
who also must have
worked together

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:10.930
started talking about GDPR.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.360
And I could not understand
why they were talking

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.170
about GDPR at a water park.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.089
And they were really
stressing out about it.

00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:19.630
They were going on and on about
how much work they had to do,

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:21.250
how many open
questions they had,

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:24.420
and how hard it was to
get a straight answer.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:27.270
And it was only later that I
realized that I was wearing

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.230
this T-shirt that says GDPR.

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:33.050
It turns out that
my GDPR T-shirt

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:34.624
ruined that couple's vacation.

00:11:34.624 --> 00:11:35.540
I felt bad about that.

00:11:38.270 --> 00:11:41.030
I worked on GDPR for
Firebase and I could relate.

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:44.054
It was a lot of work and
a fair amount of stress.

00:11:44.054 --> 00:11:45.470
But with the benefit
of hindsight,

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:47.360
I've come to view it
as an opportunity.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:49.193
It's an opportunity to
make sure that you're

00:11:49.193 --> 00:11:52.220
doing the right thing for
your users and their privacy.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:53.900
So what we're going
to talk about today

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:56.930
is how to implement best
practices for privacy

00:11:56.930 --> 00:12:00.070
and how Firebase can
help you do that.

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.710
We're going to cover these three
specific areas that I think

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:05.440
many app developers have
challenges with and are

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:07.180
particularly important today.

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:09.070
One, are the
services that you're

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:12.150
using protecting your
user's data properly?

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:16.670
Two, do you have proper
consent flows for your users?

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:20.405
And three, are you giving
users transparency and control

00:12:20.405 --> 00:12:21.030
that they need?

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:26.520
Let's talk about the first one.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:28.320
It's almost
impossible these days

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.362
to build an app without relying
on third party services.

00:12:31.362 --> 00:12:33.570
Many of you are probably
using Firebase, Google Cloud

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:37.440
Platform, and any number of
other services in your app.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:40.530
If those services have
access to user data,

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:43.350
it's really important that
you have a clear understanding

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:45.660
of what data those
services are capturing,

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:47.220
how that data is
protected, and how

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:49.120
it's used by those services.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:51.570
And it's on you as
the app developer

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.970
to ensure that all of that is
compliant with your privacy

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:58.170
policy, terms of service,
and consent experience.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:00.700
How can Firebase help with this?

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:03.540
Well, first, in order to
help you be more confident

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:06.240
in Firebase's privacy
and security practices,

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.550
we've been working hard
to get industry standard

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:10.500
certifications for Firebase.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.050
You can see that we already
have a bevy of ISO and SOC

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:15.810
certifications for
many Firebase products,

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:18.360
and we're continuing to
expand this constantly.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:23.460
You can see just recently we
added ISO 27017 and ISO 27018

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:24.840
for Firestore.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:27.610
And you'll see more of these
over the coming weeks, months,

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:30.510
and years.

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:32.590
In addition to third
party certifications,

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:36.050
something new at GDPR is
the concept of a DPST.

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:38.640
What a DPST is is a legal
agreement between you

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:42.330
and your service provider that
governs how they are protecting

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:45.030
and using your user data.

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:47.172
If you're subject to
GDPR, it's very important

00:13:47.172 --> 00:13:49.380
that you have these in place
with all of your service

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:50.910
providers.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:53.860
The good news is that Firebase
makes this really easy.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:57.080
All Firebase services
are covered under a DPST,

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.550
and that DPST is
included by default

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:00.690
in our terms of service.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:02.430
So if we're using our
products and you've

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:05.625
accepted our most recent terms,
you're covered under our DPSTs.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:10.906
OK, consent flows.

00:14:10.906 --> 00:14:12.280
I want to highlight
consent flows

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:15.500
because the standards for
consent flows are changing.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:17.830
A consent flow that
was totally fine a year

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:21.340
or two ago may not be
fine under current norms.

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:24.130
And so if you haven't
reviewed your flows recently,

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:27.390
I strongly suggest that you do.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:29.294
The overall goal
here is to make it

00:14:29.294 --> 00:14:31.710
really clear to your users
what data you're are collecting

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:34.170
about them and how
you're going to use it,

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:39.690
and getting really clear consent
for that use from your users.

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:41.190
There's been a lot
of back and forth

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:44.490
about checkboxes versus buttons.

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:46.290
The main takeaway
here is that you

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:49.680
want to get really, really
clear consent from folks.

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:53.740
Now once you have
that experience right,

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:55.220
you're not quite done yet.

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:58.240
There's a few other things that
tend to trip up developers.

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.450
One is you need to be really
careful about what data you're

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:04.305
collecting before you have
consent from your users.

00:15:04.305 --> 00:15:06.430
We see this all too often
where people accidentally

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:09.430
collect data that they shouldn't
be when their app starts,

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:10.540
for instance.

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:12.820
Be very careful that
you're not doing that.

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:15.790
Two, you need to make sure
that you're recording consent

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:17.440
in a way that you can prove it.

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:20.050
So if a regulator comes to
you down the road and says,

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:22.060
do you have consent
for user X, you

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:25.096
should be able to prove
that on your backend.

00:15:25.096 --> 00:15:27.280
So how can Firebase help?

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:29.020
First, Firebase has
done a lot of work

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:33.480
to improve your control over
how data is collected and when.

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:35.980
We have a number of services
like Crashlytics and Analytics,

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:39.640
for instance, that by default
wake up when the app wakes up

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:41.530
and starts collecting data.

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:43.390
In some cases
that's totally fine.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:46.330
In other cases you may decide
that you want to get consent

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:48.340
before that data is collected.

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:50.620
So it's now possible with
all Firebase services

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:53.930
that have this behavior to
change their default behavior.

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:56.710
So you can configure
them not to load on start

00:15:56.710 --> 00:15:58.840
and activate them
programmatically

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:02.360
when you have consent and
you're ready to gather the data.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:04.600
In addition, we've
published some example code

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:06.600
for notice and consent.

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:09.190
This includes UI for
how to get consent,

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:12.060
but also how to store
consent with Firebase

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:15.210
in a way that gives you
the tracking that you need.

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:18.001
This is available today
in our FriendlyPix app.

00:16:18.001 --> 00:16:20.250
And for those of you aren't
familiar with FriendlyPix,

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:23.070
it's an open source app
for Android, iOS, and web

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:24.870
that shows you actually
a bunch of things

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:26.600
about how to build on Firebase.

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:28.350
So I strongly suggest
that you take a look

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:31.530
at that to get a better
idea of how to get

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:35.220
this critical experience right.

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:37.770
All right, finally,
are you offering users

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:40.980
transparency and
control of their data?

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.940
The key goal here is
that your users should

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:47.050
have clear visibility into
what data you have about them

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:49.246
and that they should be
in control of that data.

00:16:49.246 --> 00:16:51.620
I've put up a screenshot here
from the Google My Activity

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:54.230
center, which I think is a
really good example of how

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:56.470
to do transparency control well.

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:58.160
This is available
to all Google users.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:16:59.534
And when you log
in, it gives you

00:16:59.534 --> 00:17:03.080
a detailed view of your usage
of Chrome, Search, Android,

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:05.082
and a bunch of other
developer tools.

00:17:05.082 --> 00:17:06.290
It's really quite exhaustive.

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:08.119
You can see it includes
individual searches

00:17:08.119 --> 00:17:10.730
that I've run on Google.

00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:12.960
In addition to giving me
a lot of transparency,

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:14.690
it also puts me in control.

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:18.050
I can delete individual records,
I can delete data in bulk,

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:22.394
I can even export data for use
outside of Google services.

00:17:22.394 --> 00:17:24.560
I think this is a really
good example of what we all

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:27.120
should be doing to provide
our users more transparency

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:29.970
and control.

00:17:29.970 --> 00:17:33.320
So how does Firebase
help with this?

00:17:33.320 --> 00:17:35.960
First, we've looked through
all of our applications

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:38.270
and all of our
services to make sure

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:40.550
that they have really
good delete support.

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:42.050
So that, for
instance, if you get

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:44.720
a request to delete a
specific user's data,

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:47.390
you can go through and delete
Crashlytics data or Analytics

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:49.597
data associated
with those users.

00:17:49.597 --> 00:17:51.930
Some of our services didn't
have the best support there,

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:53.840
and that's all been
updated recently

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:57.530
to ensure that you can
be compliant with GDPR.

00:17:57.530 --> 00:18:00.020
In addition, we've published
an export and deletion

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:03.050
guide that demonstrates
how to properly handle

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:07.220
user deletion and user export
for apps built on Firebase.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:09.680
It's actually really
quite powerful and simple

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:11.630
using the power of
Cloud Functions,

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:13.963
and so I'm going to walk you
through a little bit of how

00:18:13.963 --> 00:18:14.892
that works.

00:18:14.892 --> 00:18:16.850
Before I do, I want to
mention that this all up

00:18:16.850 --> 00:18:20.480
on GitHub in the Firebase
repository under User Privacy.

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:22.894
It's open source, so you
can grab it today and start

00:18:22.894 --> 00:18:23.810
using it in your apps.

00:18:27.784 --> 00:18:30.200
So I mentioned we're going to
use Cloud Functions in order

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:31.920
to do this stuff.

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:34.370
And what's great
about Cloud Functions

00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:37.550
is the flexibility you
have in terms of when

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:39.159
functions are kicked off.

00:18:39.159 --> 00:18:41.450
In this case, we're going to
take advantage of the fact

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:44.630
that Firebase authentication
is an event emitter.

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:47.120
And so you can see at
the top of this code

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:51.020
we're keying off of
functions.auth.user.ondelete.

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:53.400
What this means is that
when a Firebase auth

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:56.630
user gets deleted, this function
will get called and then clean

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:58.170
up the rest of the data.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:00.390
Now this is really
very flexible.

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:03.560
You can also rely on an HTTP
request to kick this off.

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:08.000
You can even use our client-side
SDK to kick off this function.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.310
Once it's initiated,
this function basically

00:19:10.310 --> 00:19:13.040
just goes through and calls
wipeout calls for each one

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:17.580
of our services in Firebase.

00:19:17.580 --> 00:19:21.080
So let's go ahead and look
at the database wipeout call.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:23.120
Once again, fairly
simple function.

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:26.990
What this does is it loads in a
number of paths from a config,

00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:28.650
and then iterates
through those paths,

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:31.830
deleting each one of
them from the database.

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:33.890
But where does the
paths come from?

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:37.160
Well, they're over here
in user_privacy.json.

00:19:37.160 --> 00:19:41.600
You can see here I specified
two paths, users and admins,

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:45.120
both in this case keyed
off of the user ID.

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:49.220
So I can know that when
my auth user gets deleted,

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:52.812
the data at these two paths
will also get deleted.

00:19:52.812 --> 00:19:54.270
This also works
for other services,

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:56.220
and I've included an
example of how this would

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:59.306
work in storage further down.

00:19:59.306 --> 00:20:00.150
So there it is.

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.700
With a few simple
functions, I can

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:04.830
rest assured that
when my Firebase auth

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:10.390
user gets deleted, their data
is out of my system as well.

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:13.632
So the key takeaway for
you is that Firebase

00:20:13.632 --> 00:20:15.090
has done a lot of
work to make sure

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:18.540
that we can meet the compliance
and regulatory needs of most

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:21.570
organizations, and that
we've done a lot of work

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:24.434
to help you be
compliant with GDPR.

00:20:24.434 --> 00:20:26.100
And with that, I'm
going to hand it over

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:28.433
to Mike, who is going to talk
about taking your Firebase

00:20:28.433 --> 00:20:30.210
app to production.

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:32.300
MICHAEL MCDONALD: Thanks, John.

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:36.410
OK, so John just
showed functions code

00:20:36.410 --> 00:20:38.000
that needs to get
deployed somewhere.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.132
He showed databases
and storage buckets.

00:20:40.132 --> 00:20:41.840
How do we know that
that code is actually

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:44.220
going to scale once
it's out in production?

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:47.000
And once it's out there, how do
we know that John didn't just

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:49.220
copy those blindly
from a GitHub repo

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:51.670
and deploy them and
assume that they work?

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:54.320
We need to verify that our
functions aren't crashing,

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:56.270
that code is actually
running and doing

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:57.187
what we think it will.

00:20:57.187 --> 00:20:59.144
And lastly, we have to
make sure that we're not

00:20:59.144 --> 00:21:00.470
breaking the bank.

00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:02.960
We don't want someone
from finance crawling up

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:04.610
into our cubicle
and saying, you're

00:21:04.610 --> 00:21:07.130
spending way too much money,
you need to shut that down.

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:09.900
Because that's a critical
part of your business.

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:12.300
So let's tackle
those individually.

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:14.910
Because Firebase is
built on top of Cloud,

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:18.060
it can both scale to meet
those incredible needs

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:19.780
of your organization.

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.120
But with tools like
Functions and serverless,

00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:27.000
we're also making sure that
we can do it economically.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:28.940
So we'll start with scale.

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:32.300
A lot of Firebase features like
Cloud Firestore, Cloud Storage,

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:34.850
and Cloud Functions,
as the name implies,

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:37.220
are built on top
of Google Cloud.

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:39.350
So you can use the
Firebase client

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:42.020
SDKs and our really,
really nice console

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:44.125
to maximize developer
productivity.

00:21:44.125 --> 00:21:45.500
And then if you
need to do things

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:48.410
like automate the creation
of storage buckets

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:51.210
or create different environments
for all of your functions,

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:53.930
you can also use the
power of the various tools

00:21:53.930 --> 00:21:55.820
that Google Cloud
provides in order

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:59.990
to supercharge and provide
the granular customizability

00:21:59.990 --> 00:22:02.360
that you need.

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:04.490
One of the other
things because of that

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:06.950
is that a lot of Firebase
services like those

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:08.760
are available in other regions.

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:10.610
So Cloud Storage,
for instance, you

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:13.167
can create buckets in
all of these regions.

00:22:13.167 --> 00:22:14.750
And actually it's
probably out of date

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:17.660
because I bet we probably just
launched new regions today

00:22:17.660 --> 00:22:19.922
or sometime in the recent past.

00:22:19.922 --> 00:22:21.380
We have a ton of
different regions,

00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:24.710
so can you meet either
compliance needs or performance

00:22:24.710 --> 00:22:25.250
needs.

00:22:25.250 --> 00:22:27.840
If you have users in
Australia, for instance,

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:29.810
you might want to have
buckets in Australia

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:34.010
to meet either the Australian
data locality requirements

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:37.530
or just simple performance.

00:22:37.530 --> 00:22:41.130
Along that line, today
or in the recent past

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:43.890
or in the near future,
Cloud Functions

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:45.986
will be offering
regionality support.

00:22:45.986 --> 00:22:47.610
So you can actually
take your functions

00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:50.460
and deploy them exactly
where your users are.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:57.050
You can just type in --region
and deploy to whatever region.

00:22:57.050 --> 00:22:59.400
This will be coming
for Cloud Firestore

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:00.570
as well in the future.

00:23:03.410 --> 00:23:06.171
So we've deployed all of
our various resources.

00:23:06.171 --> 00:23:08.420
How do we know that those
resources are actually doing

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:10.650
what we say they're doing?

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.410
So that's where
Stackdriver comes in.

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:15.530
So Stackdriver is not only
Google's monitoring solution,

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:19.670
but it will also do alerting,
it will do error reporting.

00:23:19.670 --> 00:23:21.500
Basically, it's
the one-stop shop

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:25.180
for your operational needs.

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:27.859
Firebase has a number of
integrations with Stackdriver.

00:23:27.859 --> 00:23:29.650
So you can take a look
at the various cloud

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:33.220
integrated services and see
which pieces of Stackdriver

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:34.630
are currently included.

00:23:34.630 --> 00:23:37.110
So a ton of things
kick out metrics

00:23:37.110 --> 00:23:38.320
to Stackdriver monitoring.

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:40.792
And therefore you can alert
based on changes to those.

00:23:40.792 --> 00:23:43.000
And then Cloud Functions
provides really nice logging

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:47.000
and error reporting
that I'll demo later.

00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:51.240
So we'll show it right now, and
then we'll demo it in a minute.

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:53.794
But John went and deployed
a bunch of functions.

00:23:53.794 --> 00:23:55.210
FriendlyPix, I
think, actually has

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:57.280
about 15 different
functions running

00:23:57.280 --> 00:24:01.420
in the background for everything
from cleaning up user accounts

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:03.760
after they've been
deleted to it's

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:06.250
a pictures on the internet app.

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:08.790
So what are people going to
post on the internet-- horrible,

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:10.990
horrible ridiculous content.

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:14.800
So we actually have a function
that runs the Cloud Vision API,

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:18.940
and it will pull in the photo,
do an NSFW or inappropriate

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:20.950
content filtering on
it, and then decide

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:22.420
whether or not to moderate it.

00:24:22.420 --> 00:24:24.550
And again, that's a
business critical thing.

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:29.420
So look up here, make sure
that nothing is erroring out.

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:31.760
And on the case that
something is erroring out,

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:35.000
we can actually dive into
Stackdriver Error Reporting

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:38.660
and take a look at exactly
what error is occurring.

00:24:38.660 --> 00:24:41.060
So if you notice here,
we can see things

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:42.500
like permission denied.

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:45.290
I couldn't write to the database
because something happened.

00:24:45.290 --> 00:24:47.207
Or you can actually see,
if you look at mine,

00:24:47.207 --> 00:24:49.040
that I don't know how
to program JavaScript.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:51.256
And so I got a bunch of
crazy JavaScript errors

00:24:51.256 --> 00:24:52.880
that are basically
just like, hey, Mike

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:54.320
doesn't know how to program.

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:57.980
But you can start doing things
like opening or closing those

00:24:57.980 --> 00:25:00.440
or acknowledging them that
someone is working on it,

00:25:00.440 --> 00:25:01.850
and track that.

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:03.350
So we'll demo that
right at the end.

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:10.020
So we've verified
logs are looking good.

00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:12.260
We're getting hopefully
no errors showing up

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:13.352
in error reporting.

00:25:13.352 --> 00:25:14.810
Now we want longer
term monitoring.

00:25:14.810 --> 00:25:16.940
So we want to make
sure that the cloud is

00:25:16.940 --> 00:25:18.542
up and still responding.

00:25:18.542 --> 00:25:20.000
But we also may
want to know things

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:23.390
like how much data is being
added to our storage bucket.

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:26.000
So how many pictures
are being added per day.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:28.910
I want to know because finance
has told me that I can only

00:25:28.910 --> 00:25:30.830
spend $100 a month on this app.

00:25:30.830 --> 00:25:33.260
I want to start making
sure that I'm not

00:25:33.260 --> 00:25:35.750
storing more than 100
gigabytes of data.

00:25:35.750 --> 00:25:39.140
I can use Stackdriver monitoring
to start setting alerts

00:25:39.140 --> 00:25:41.540
and understanding, maybe
put up a big dashboard

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:44.660
for the entire team to see
what growth looks like,

00:25:44.660 --> 00:25:46.390
and start sending
alerts based on that.

00:25:46.390 --> 00:25:49.550
A bunch of different Firebase
products offer a ton, a ton,

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:51.700
a ton of different
metrics on this.

00:25:54.290 --> 00:25:56.905
And as I mentioned, you can
then, through Stackdriver,

00:25:56.905 --> 00:25:59.340
to start dashboarding
these things.

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:01.850
So this is a dashboard that
I built for FriendlyPix

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:03.260
that has data stored.

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:05.120
So I set a five gigabyte alert.

00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:08.640
So five gigabytes I think is
the free-tier limit for storage.

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:10.850
So I said, hey, I want to
draw a line across that

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:12.590
and make sure that
I'm not getting close,

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:14.630
and then set up an
alert based on that.

00:26:14.630 --> 00:26:18.110
Similarly, I want to track
other key metrics like bandwidth

00:26:18.110 --> 00:26:22.965
or requests coming into
the database in storage.

00:26:22.965 --> 00:26:25.600
And as I said, I can
then set up policies

00:26:25.600 --> 00:26:27.170
based on those metrics.

00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:30.130
So this is my alert
for five gigabytes.

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:31.930
So you can see--

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:33.970
let me know any
time the total bytes

00:26:33.970 --> 00:26:37.480
metric is above five gigabytes
for more than one minute.

00:26:37.480 --> 00:26:40.100
And I will send that
email to Firebase Mike.

00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:42.190
Though I could if I
wanted to, and actually

00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:44.710
have services running that
send me really annoying text

00:26:44.710 --> 00:26:47.290
messages if my "Minecraft"
server goes down,

00:26:47.290 --> 00:26:49.990
just to make sure that
everything is up and running.

00:26:49.990 --> 00:26:52.030
And these alerts are
actually great because you

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:54.150
can add markdown documentation.

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:56.080
So for instance,
maybe you already

00:26:56.080 --> 00:26:57.980
have a playbook
for your service.

00:26:57.980 --> 00:27:00.910
You can actually drop that
playbook documentation directly

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:01.570
into it.

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:03.528
So whenever I get an
email where my "Minecraft"

00:27:03.528 --> 00:27:06.067
server goes down, it actually
has exactly the three steps

00:27:06.067 --> 00:27:07.900
that I usually just
have to copy and paste--

00:27:07.900 --> 00:27:09.820
because I haven't automated it--

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:13.990
directly into SSH into the
machine, fix it, and I'm done.

00:27:13.990 --> 00:27:15.370
So you get everything there.

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:16.953
And then you get
another email saying,

00:27:16.953 --> 00:27:20.890
hey, we've cleaned up the issue,
everything is good to go again.

00:27:23.460 --> 00:27:26.310
And this is actually an
exact example of that right

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:29.640
where you see, hey,
"Minecraft" server is failing.

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:32.320
Right down at the bottom we
have the policy documentation

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:34.680
that is literally just, hey,
make sure the VM is still

00:27:34.680 --> 00:27:36.450
running, mount
whatever appropriate

00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:38.950
disks, et cetera, et cetera.

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:40.890
And then when it's
fixed, you can see, hey,

00:27:40.890 --> 00:27:43.440
the violation is ended,
everything is all clear.

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:48.380
The last thing, we
talked about all

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:51.020
of those finance people
cropping up in unexpected times

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:54.170
right when your service
is on its stellar growth.

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:56.960
You need to make sure
that, one, you can estimate

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:58.320
the cost of your service.

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:00.440
So we've provided
cost calculators

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:02.540
through both Firebase
and Google Cloud

00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:05.359
to understand, hey, maybe
you're building a chat app

00:28:05.359 --> 00:28:07.400
and you want to send a
certain number of messages

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:10.070
or you're storing a
certain number of pictures.

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:11.870
We can let you
slide around and see

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:13.520
approximately what
it's going to cost

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:16.860
to run on our infrastructure.

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:19.490
And because you want to
ensure that once your thing is

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:22.634
running, you're not going
to just completely blow

00:28:22.634 --> 00:28:24.050
your budget away,
you can actually

00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:25.460
start setting budget alerts.

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:27.950
So I set a budget
alert here that I

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:30.080
don't want to spend
any more than $50

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.630
a month running my
"Minecraft" server.

00:28:32.630 --> 00:28:38.360
So I set at 50%, send me an
alert at 90% and at 100%,

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:41.720
send me an email that just
says, hey, Mike, FYI, you're

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:43.030
coming up on your budget alert.

00:28:43.030 --> 00:28:45.530
And one of the things John and
I were actually talking right

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:50.360
before this talk, we've created
this fun meta recursive system

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:52.670
where you can actually
have billing alerts trigger

00:28:52.670 --> 00:28:55.790
functions that would
then let you say shut off

00:28:55.790 --> 00:28:59.000
resources, which is fun
because those functions also

00:28:59.000 --> 00:28:59.600
cost money.

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:01.970
And it would end up
in this weird world

00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:03.889
where who knows what
you could end up doing.

00:29:03.889 --> 00:29:05.930
But that's the system that
we're trying to build.

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:07.880
We're giving you all the tools.

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:09.320
And then in Firebase
we're working

00:29:09.320 --> 00:29:11.150
to automate that
and orchestrate it

00:29:11.150 --> 00:29:15.860
to give you really the control
you need at a high level.

00:29:15.860 --> 00:29:19.170
So let's switch
over to the demo.

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:24.050
And I will actually show you
by building a couple of things

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:25.320
how this all works.

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:29.570
So here we're looking at
the backend for FriendlyPix.

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:33.710
And hopefully everyone can see
that in the back of the room.

00:29:33.710 --> 00:29:35.870
But you can see a bunch
of logs that we have,

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:37.700
and you can start
seeing things like,

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:39.050
oh, we're getting some warning.

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:42.910
So maybe I want to
actually dive into that,

00:29:42.910 --> 00:29:45.580
and we can just
search for warning.

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:49.930
And I can say, load more
logs, load older logs,

00:29:49.930 --> 00:29:52.270
scroll through, et cetera.

00:29:52.270 --> 00:29:55.270
And there are actually a ton
of things that give out logs.

00:29:55.270 --> 00:29:58.180
So I can for instance look
at various audited resources

00:29:58.180 --> 00:30:01.600
and say, hey, I want to know
everything calling the Vision

00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:04.780
API, or I want to know all of
the changes that have been made

00:30:04.780 --> 00:30:07.000
to the Google OAuth Client ID.

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:10.150
And a bunch of that
information also shows up.

00:30:10.150 --> 00:30:13.540
And if I want, I could also dive
into say particular functions.

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:15.550
And maybe I know that
one of these functions

00:30:15.550 --> 00:30:16.780
is being problematic--

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:18.490
I want to look for logs on that.

00:30:18.490 --> 00:30:21.730
Not only can I filter
by doing arbitrary text,

00:30:21.730 --> 00:30:24.820
I can filter by any of
these other columns.

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:28.525
Maybe only give me critical
errors in all functions,

00:30:28.525 --> 00:30:30.400
and hopefully in the
last hour there have not

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:31.940
been any critical errors.

00:30:31.940 --> 00:30:36.610
Though we'll let it load and
see if that is actually true.

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:38.840
If there are, I recommend
deleting FriendlyPix.

00:30:38.840 --> 00:30:40.419
No.

00:30:40.419 --> 00:30:40.960
There you go.

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:43.650
So nothing is horribly broken
in FriendlyPix right now.

00:30:43.650 --> 00:30:46.680
And if it was-- so actually
we did this talk at I/O

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:48.670
and we were having
errors, so I immediately

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:50.830
added all sorts of alerting.

00:30:50.830 --> 00:30:52.690
And now the
